  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 AM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nA surface ridge axis was overhead last evening and is now\nshifting to the south allowing southerly winds to develop. \nTemperatures early this morning have dropped into the upper 20s \nto mid 30s, but are holding steady now and some are beginning to\nrise across western MN. The growing season has ended for much of\nthe region. The southerly winds will increase markedly after\nsunrise and forecast soundings indicate some gusts of 25 to 30\nmph are likely by late morning and afternoon. Temps will rebound\nquickly with the increased wind later this morning and highs \nwill reach the upper 50s to mid 60s this afternoon. The wind \ncombined with RH in the mid 20s to mid 30s will lead to \nelevated fire weather conditions, especially in western MN where\nthe strongest winds and lowest RH will reside. A near repeat is\nexpected for Sunday, although temperatures will warm to the mid\nto upper 60s ahead of a cold front reaching central MN in the \nafternoon. The front will also bring lighter winds along it, so\nthe breezy conditions will be confined to southern MN and \nwestern WI.\n\nAfter slightly cooler temperatures Monday, a warming trend is\nexpected to prevail through the rest of the week. The pattern \nwill feature a ridge across much of the CONUS with troughs on \nthe east and west coasts. Very quiet and warm weather will \nresult and temperatures well into the 70s are expected from\nmidweek onward, which would be 15 to 20 degrees above normal. A\nweak short wave passing through Wednesday will be dry with the \nonly chances for rain coming next weekend as the western long \nwave trough finally begins to approach.",
